            <ArticleTitle>A pilot study of the effects of niacin administration on free fatty acid and growth hormone concentrations in children with obesity.</ArticleTitle>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1111/ijpo.12184</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="CONTEXT" NlmCategory="BACKGROUND">Children with obesity have low spontaneous growth hormone (GH) secretion. High circulating free fatty acid (FFA) concentration is believed to inhibit GH secretion in those with obesity. In adults, lipolytic inhibition with niacin lowers FFA and increases GH, but there are no prior studies in children with obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">The objective of the study was to determine the dose and frequency of niacin administration required to lower FFA and stimulate GH in children with obesity.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Dose-finding study of nondiabetic children ages 6-12 years with body mass index (BMI) ≥ 95th percentile given niacin 250 mg q2h × 3 doses (n = 2), 500 mg q2h × 3 doses (n = 5) or 500 mg q1h × 4 doses (n = 5).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PARTICIPANTS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Eight boys and four girls (age 9.7 ± 1.8 years; BMI 26.4 ± 3.1 kg m(-2) ; BMIz 2.2 ± .25) were studied.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Percentage of serum FFA values that were below 0.2 mEq L(-1) . GH, insulin and glucose were also measured serially.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">FFA decreased as the dose and frequency of niacin increased (p = .01). Niacin 500 mg q1h 4 doses suppressed FFA &lt; 0.2 mEq L(-1) and significantly increased GH (p = .04). Adverse effects were flushing/warmth (100%), tingling (60%) and GI complaints (20-40%).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Niacin 500 mg q1h significantly lowered serum FFA and increased GH. These pilot data suggest that high FFA is an important suppressor of GH secretion in children with obesity.</AbstractText>
                <CopyrightInformation>Published 2016. This article is a U.S. Government work and is in the public domain in the USA.</CopyrightInformation>
            </Abstract>
